# A worker for controller.ic10. Two housings run this same file, so it has two
# threads in the Call Stack: workerA and workerB. Each housing's Setting says
# which slot of the controller's stack it reports to (1 or 2); its
# acknowledgement goes two slots further on.
# Things to try:
# - A breakpoint on "mul" stops for both workers, in turn. Click a thread to see
#   its registers: Job differs between them.
# - Edit the breakpoint's condition to db.Setting == 2: it stops only for workerB.
# - Inline values on the get and put lines show the entries they address.
alias Controller d0
alias Slot r0
alias Job r1
alias Result r2
alias Last r3
alias Ack r4
l Slot db Setting
add Ack Slot 2
move Last 0 # no job answered yet
loop:
get Job db 0 # the job the controller put in this chip's stack
beq Job Last idle # the same job as last time: nothing to do
sleep Slot # pretend the work takes a while: workerA 1 s, workerB 2 s
mul Result Job Job
put Controller Slot Result # the answer, in the controller's stack
put Controller Ack Job # and which job it answers, so the controller knows
move Last Job
idle:
yield
j loop
